Let \[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
\sqrt{x} &\text{ if }x>4, \\
x^2 &\text{ if }x \le 4.
\end{array}
\right.\]Find $f(f(f(2)))$.

output:
If the area of the square is 325 square meters, then the length of each side is $\sqrt{325} = \sqrt{5^2 \cdot 13} = 5\sqrt{13}$ meters.
The perimeter of the square is 4 times the length of one side, so it is $4 \cdot 5\sqrt{13} = \boxed{20\sqrt{13}}$ meters.
The answer is: 20\sqrt{13}

